00:34:36.875 --> 00:34:40.958
Okay, so, how did Flat Earth actually gain traction?

00:34:40.958 --> 00:34:45.416
Like, okay, there’s a lot of different conspiracy theories 
out there, there’s a lot of worldviews,

00:34:45.416 --> 00:34:53.416
both legitimate and scams, that have the same “you have been
 lied to” pitch. So why Flat Earth?

00:34:53.416 --> 00:34:57.000
Modern Flat Earth was essentially created by content 
algorithms

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:02.875
trying to maximize retention and engagement by serving users
 suggestions for things that are,

00:35:02.875 --> 00:35:08.750
effectively, incrementally more concentrated versions of the
 thing they were already looking at.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:14.666
Bizarre cranks peddling random theories are an aspect of 
civilization that has always been with us,

00:35:14.666 --> 00:35:21.375
so it was inevitable that they would end up on YouTube, but 
the algorithm made sure they found an audience.

00:35:21.375 --> 00:35:26.750
These systems were accidentally identifying people 
susceptible to conspiratorial

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:32.166
and reactionary thinking and sending them increasingly 
deeper into Flat Earth evangelism.

00:35:32.166 --> 00:35:35.625
And within that was just a quirk of good timing, I guess.

00:35:35.625 --> 00:35:41.875
The right cranks were churning out huge volumes of 
low-effort content at just the right time for YouTube

00:35:41.875 --> 00:35:48.625
to prioritize it, and the moment it gained any traction at 
all the system reinforced that success.

00:35:48.625 --> 00:35:51.666
But, you know, let’s keep some perspective here.

00:35:51.666 --> 00:35:55.750
Flat Earth, for all the command it holds over our collective
 interest,

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:58.041
is still a very small movement.

00:35:58.041 --> 00:36:05.416
The largest channels cap out around 150,000 subscribers, 
with most coming in well below that.

00:36:05.416 --> 00:36:12.541
and this is not much in the scope of YouTube where the 
opportunity cost of subscription is extremely low,

00:36:12.541 --> 00:36:17.541
and for the last couple years newer pro-flat-earth videos 
from these channels

00:36:17.541 --> 00:36:22.083
have mostly been skating by with a few thousand views.

00:36:22.083 --> 00:36:27.083
In fact Mark Sargent, the guy largely credited with 
popularizing Flat Earth in the YouTube era,

00:36:27.083 --> 00:36:31.625
his videos used to get hundreds of thousands of views.

00:36:31.625 --> 00:36:35.791
His seminal work, Flat Earth Clues, has 1.1 million views.

00:36:35.791 --> 00:36:41.000
But he’s only had two videos break six digits since the 
start of 2018:

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:48.916
one that’s just a bootleg repost of Shane Dawson’s Flat 
Earth video, and one whinging about The Last Jedi.

00:36:48.916 --> 00:36:54.458
Now, YouTube, notably, has made some changes to the 
algorithm to prioritize credible sources,

00:36:54.458 --> 00:36:58.916
which lowers the ranking of Flat Earth videos even within 
their own sphere,

00:36:58.916 --> 00:37:06.041
but those changes were only implemented in early 2019 when 
Flat Earth was already on a downslope.

